# Hostile audit — isolate-free terminal rhombic grid through `t<=1.8687`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_isolate_free_terminal_rhombic_grid_t18687_2026-09-15.md`  
**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only scope.**

The candidate only composes two previously proved statements:

1. every bounded contact face meeting the retained terminal-free corridor is a strict rhombus;
2. every bounded contact face containing a contact-isolated source point has degree at least seven.

The possible loopholes are boundary placement and whether an isolate must belong to a bounded face.

## Boundary audit

The retained run is chosen from internal ladder cells and is disjoint from the global outer-boundary sides. A source point in the open run therefore cannot lie in the unbounded face. Since it is contact-isolated, it is not a contact-graph vertex or edge point, so it lies in the interior of a unique bounded face of the embedded contact graph.

## Face-degree audit

The parent corridor theorem excludes bounded nonquadrilateral faces from the run. By the canonical quadrilateral theorem, every bounded quadrilateral face there is a strict unit rhombus, hence degree four.

The isolate-host theorem uses strict inequalities `|z-v_i|>1`, valid because `z` has no contact edge. Its central-angle proof gives strict `theta_i<pi/3`, so even a hexagonal host is impossible; degree at least seven is exact.

Thus an isolate in the retained run would force the same bounded face to have degree four and degree at least seven. This is impossible.

No claim is made that the corridor has no contact-graph vertices: the rhombic grid vertices remain source points. The proved statement is exactly that there are no **contact-isolated** source points in the open corridor and hence no hidden source points in strict-rhombus interiors.

**Audit disposition:** PASS. The previous caveat allowing isolates inside retained rhombi can be removed.
